1
00:00:00,670 --> 00:00:02,620
This mammoth may have had

2
00:00:02,620 --> 00:00:04,080
an extraordinary coat.

3
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:06,530
Scientists found two mutations

4
00:00:06,530 --> 00:00:07,870
in a gene linked

5
00:00:07,870 --> 00:00:09,040
to hair development.

6
00:00:09,490 --> 00:00:11,230
They predicted a silky coat,

7
00:00:11,670 --> 00:00:13,430
but could not observe it directly.

8
00:00:13,710 --> 00:00:15,470
The animal lived on Wrangel Island,

9
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:16,920
in the Arctic Ocean,

10
00:00:17,290 --> 00:00:18,630
a few thousand years ago.

11
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:20,060
Now the strange part.

12
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,350
A later study of ancient genomes

13
00:00:22,610 --> 00:00:24,150
found that the island population

14
00:00:24,150 --> 00:00:26,700
recovered after an extreme bottleneck.

15
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:29,000
Yet the mammoths eventually disappeared.

16
00:00:29,260 --> 00:00:30,830
How can both things be true?

17
00:00:31,220 --> 00:00:33,390
Scientists have examined genetic damage,

18
00:00:33,670 --> 00:00:35,570
climate, and human arrival,

19
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:37,480
but the final generations

20
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:39,310
are missing from the genetic record.

21
00:00:39,690 --> 00:00:41,880
So which explanation holds up?

22
00:00:42,110 --> 00:00:43,580
We will take the evidence apart,

23
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:44,780
and at the end,

24
00:00:45,070 --> 00:00:46,470
separate what is proven,

25
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:47,840
what is plausible,

26
00:00:48,190 --> 00:00:49,290
and what is still missing

27
00:00:49,290 --> 00:00:49,970
from the file.

28
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:03,050
Around ten thousand years ago, sea levels

29
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:05,630
rose and the Arctic coastline moved.

30
00:01:06,020 --> 00:01:09,210
A population of mammoths became isolated on

31
00:01:09,210 --> 00:01:12,170
Wrangel Island, with no easy route back

32
00:01:12,170 --> 00:01:14,390
to the mainland. They survived there for

33
00:01:14,390 --> 00:01:17,160
thousands of years, among the last mammoths

34
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:20,770
on Earth.  Other populations disappeared earlier, and

35
00:01:20,770 --> 00:01:23,900
differently. Mammoths on the East European plains

36
00:01:24,170 --> 00:01:26,770
vanished roughly fifteen thousand years ago.

37
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:28,640
The population on St.

38
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:31,540
Paul Island in Alaska lasted until about

39
00:01:31,540 --> 00:01:33,090
fifty six hundred years ago.

40
00:01:33,390 --> 00:01:35,870
Studies of their remains found changes in

41
00:01:35,870 --> 00:01:39,260
isotope signatures toward the end, consistent with

42
00:01:39,260 --> 00:01:42,890
changing environments.  On Wrangel, the story looked

43
00:01:42,890 --> 00:01:45,090
different. A 2019 isotope study found that the

44
00:01:45,090 --> 00:01:50,540
animals' collagen carbon and nitrogen values remained

45
00:01:50,540 --> 00:01:52,870
broadly stable across the sampled period.

46
00:01:53,260 --> 00:01:55,440
That does not prove every season was

47
00:01:55,440 --> 00:01:57,360
easy. But it makes the ending a

48
00:01:57,360 --> 00:01:59,700
mystery: the signals did not show the

49
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:03,060
clear gradual dietary decline the researchers expected.

50
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:06,300
A population survived isolation for millennia.

51
00:02:06,710 --> 00:02:07,740
And then it disappeared.

52
00:02:08,935 --> 00:02:10,845
Our first witness is DNA.

53
00:02:11,155 --> 00:02:15,855
In 2017, researchers compared two woolly mammoth genomes:

54
00:02:16,295 --> 00:02:19,515
one from mainland Siberia, around forty five

55
00:02:19,515 --> 00:02:22,235
thousand years old, and one from Wrangel

56
00:02:22,235 --> 00:02:25,135
Island, around forty three hundred years old.

57
00:02:25,455 --> 00:02:27,825
The island animal carried an excess of

58
00:02:27,825 --> 00:02:30,825
genetic changes that could disrupt genes:

59
00:02:31,285 --> 00:02:35,345
more deletions, more deletions affecting genes, and

60
00:02:35,345 --> 00:02:37,185
more premature stop signals.

61
00:02:37,475 --> 00:02:40,135
The researchers interpreted the pattern as consistent

62
00:02:40,425 --> 00:02:43,415
with genomic meltdown: in a small population,

63
00:02:43,805 --> 00:02:46,675
selection can become less effective at removing

64
00:02:46,675 --> 00:02:50,065
harmful variants. The island mammoth's estimated effective

65
00:02:50,065 --> 00:02:52,845
population size was more than forty three

66
00:02:52,845 --> 00:02:54,865
times lower than the mainland mammoth's.

67
00:02:55,275 --> 00:02:58,105
Effective population size is a genetic estimate,

68
00:02:58,475 --> 00:02:59,945
not a literal headcount.

69
00:03:00,375 --> 00:03:02,405
This was a striking warning sign.

70
00:03:02,855 --> 00:03:04,995
But one island genome cannot tell us

71
00:03:04,995 --> 00:03:08,295
exactly how every individual lived, or establish

72
00:03:08,295 --> 00:03:09,635
the cause of the extinction.

73
00:03:10,065 --> 00:03:12,815
And one unusual result brought the mystery

74
00:03:12,815 --> 00:03:14,435
close to the animal itself.

75
00:03:15,623 --> 00:03:18,323
The Wrangel genome had two independent loss

76
00:03:18,323 --> 00:03:20,753
of function mutations at a gene called

77
00:03:20,753 --> 00:03:23,703
FOX Q one. Based on comparisons with

78
00:03:23,703 --> 00:03:26,383
other animals, the authors predicted a satin

79
00:03:26,383 --> 00:03:28,473
like coat. They did not observe the

80
00:03:28,473 --> 00:03:31,353
mammoth's coat directly. A predicted change in

81
00:03:31,353 --> 00:03:33,193
hair structure is not proof that the

82
00:03:33,193 --> 00:03:35,153
animal lost its wool or froze.

83
00:03:35,443 --> 00:03:36,543
Think about the difference.

84
00:03:36,993 --> 00:03:39,503
A single genome can preserve the molecular

85
00:03:39,503 --> 00:03:42,123
trace of a possible trait, long after

86
00:03:42,123 --> 00:03:43,163
the animal is gone.

87
00:03:43,573 --> 00:03:45,233
But the trace is not the same

88
00:03:45,233 --> 00:03:46,793
as seeing that trait in life.

89
00:03:47,263 --> 00:03:50,113
That distinction matters throughout this investigation.

90
00:03:50,523 --> 00:03:54,123
In 2017, the genome offered an important picture

91
00:03:54,123 --> 00:03:56,913
of genetic damage. Seven years later, a

92
00:03:56,913 --> 00:03:59,253
much larger set of genomes would reveal

93
00:03:59,253 --> 00:04:01,543
another part of the same history, and

94
00:04:01,543 --> 00:04:03,933
challenge a simple story of a herd

95
00:04:03,933 --> 00:04:05,073
slowly collapsing.

96
00:04:06,192 --> 00:04:08,502
The second witness is chemistry.

97
00:04:08,902 --> 00:04:12,122
Carbon and nitrogen isotopes in mammoth collagen

98
00:04:12,492 --> 00:04:14,042
carry clues about diet.

99
00:04:14,492 --> 00:04:17,252
Sulfur and strontium can provide clues about

100
00:04:17,252 --> 00:04:19,282
water and local geology.

101
00:04:19,652 --> 00:04:23,932
In 2019, researchers compared samples from Wrangel and

102
00:04:23,932 --> 00:04:26,772
other northern regions.  The Wrangel carbon and

103
00:04:26,772 --> 00:04:29,222
nitrogen values remained broadly stable in the

104
00:04:29,222 --> 00:04:31,832
samples they examined. That is evidence against

105
00:04:31,832 --> 00:04:35,092
some kinds of gradual dietary deterioration, not

106
00:04:35,092 --> 00:04:36,682
proof the animals were never hungry.

107
00:04:37,022 --> 00:04:40,392
Other mammoth populations showed different isotopic changes

108
00:04:40,532 --> 00:04:43,302
before they disappeared.  The team also found

109
00:04:43,302 --> 00:04:46,672
sulfur and strontium patterns consistent with increased

110
00:04:46,672 --> 00:04:48,552
bedrock weathering toward the end of the

111
00:04:48,552 --> 00:04:52,102
island population's existence. The authors suggested this

112
00:04:52,102 --> 00:04:54,032
might have affected drinking water quality.

113
00:04:54,442 --> 00:04:56,872
It is an interpretation, not a measured

114
00:04:56,872 --> 00:04:58,002
glass of bad water.

115
00:04:58,462 --> 00:05:00,322
Then there is a possible sudden threat:

116
00:05:00,772 --> 00:05:03,792
rain falling on snow, freezing into ice,

117
00:05:04,202 --> 00:05:06,292
and making plants difficult to reach.

118
00:05:06,702 --> 00:05:08,992
Such an icing event could damage a

119
00:05:08,992 --> 00:05:11,582
small herd quickly. But the study did

120
00:05:11,582 --> 00:05:13,222
not witness the fatal winter.

121
00:05:13,582 --> 00:05:16,222
Ice, water, and genetic strain form a

122
00:05:16,222 --> 00:05:19,372
plausible scenario, not a solved case.

123
00:05:20,559 --> 00:05:22,259
The third witness is us.

124
00:05:22,749 --> 00:05:25,239
Humans hunted mammoths elsewhere, so it is

125
00:05:25,239 --> 00:05:27,579
reasonable to ask whether people reached Wrangel

126
00:05:27,839 --> 00:05:29,379
while mammoths were still alive.

127
00:05:29,779 --> 00:05:32,699
The earliest known archaeological evidence of humans

128
00:05:32,699 --> 00:05:34,779
on the island dates to around thirty

129
00:05:34,779 --> 00:05:38,319
six hundred years before present, several centuries

130
00:05:38,459 --> 00:05:40,399
after the youngest mammoth remains.

131
00:05:40,819 --> 00:05:43,449
That is the earliest evidence we have,

132
00:05:43,909 --> 00:05:45,629
not a perfect record of the first

133
00:05:45,629 --> 00:05:49,719
person to arrive. The 2019 researchers said a

134
00:05:49,719 --> 00:05:52,749
human contribution cannot be ruled out, because

135
00:05:52,749 --> 00:05:55,379
finding direct evidence of an encounter would

136
00:05:55,379 --> 00:05:58,729
be difficult.  So the distinction is important.

137
00:05:59,169 --> 00:06:01,619
Human involvement is possible;

138
00:06:02,139 --> 00:06:04,779
the known dates do not establish it.

139
00:06:05,029 --> 00:06:07,359
The apparent gap is another reason not

140
00:06:07,359 --> 00:06:09,289
to turn a suspect into a verdict.

141
00:06:10,411 --> 00:06:12,861
In 2024, the fourth witness arrived:

142
00:06:12,861 --> 00:06:16,431
a study of twenty one Siberian woolly

143
00:06:16,431 --> 00:06:19,881
mammoth genomes, including fourteen from the island

144
00:06:19,881 --> 00:06:23,351
population and seven earlier mainland animals.

145
00:06:23,711 --> 00:06:26,451
Together, they gave researchers a much wider

146
00:06:26,451 --> 00:06:28,171
window into the genetic history.

147
00:06:28,481 --> 00:06:29,741
The bottleneck was real.

148
00:06:30,201 --> 00:06:34,181
One measure of genetic diversity, heterozygosity, fell

149
00:06:34,181 --> 00:06:36,881
by more than forty percent after isolation.

150
00:06:37,211 --> 00:06:40,321
Long runs of homozygosity, stretches where an

151
00:06:40,321 --> 00:06:44,021
animal carries matching inherited copies, increased from

152
00:06:44,021 --> 00:06:45,931
roughly ten to forty two percent of

153
00:06:45,931 --> 00:06:49,631
the genome. Diversity in the major histocompatibility

154
00:06:49,631 --> 00:06:52,431
complex, a group of genes involved in

155
00:06:52,431 --> 00:06:55,701
immune responses, fell more than genome-wide

156
00:06:55,701 --> 00:06:58,621
diversity.  Those are serious genetic changes.

157
00:06:59,061 --> 00:07:01,361
But a different signal complicated the story.

158
00:07:01,851 --> 00:07:05,451
Modelling suggested that effective population size dropped

159
00:07:05,451 --> 00:07:07,481
to eight or fewer at the bottleneck,

160
00:07:07,881 --> 00:07:10,191
then recovered to around two hundred to

161
00:07:10,191 --> 00:07:13,351
three hundred within roughly twenty generations.

162
00:07:13,781 --> 00:07:16,601
Again, that is an effective genetic population

163
00:07:16,601 --> 00:07:19,521
size, not a count of individual mammoths.

164
00:07:19,911 --> 00:07:22,931
The population appeared comparatively stable across the

165
00:07:22,931 --> 00:07:25,021
period represented by the samples.

166
00:07:26,108 --> 00:07:28,778
Recovery did not erase the genetic consequences

167
00:07:28,778 --> 00:07:33,448
of isolation. The 2024 researchers looked at predicted

168
00:07:33,448 --> 00:07:37,008
harmful mutations, taking care with a technical

169
00:07:37,008 --> 00:07:39,798
problem: genomes differ in how much of

170
00:07:39,798 --> 00:07:41,268
their sequence can be read.

171
00:07:41,728 --> 00:07:44,698
Imagine comparing damage in two rooms through

172
00:07:44,698 --> 00:07:46,298
windows of different sizes.

173
00:07:46,778 --> 00:07:48,528
A bigger window lets you see more,

174
00:07:48,998 --> 00:07:51,138
even when the rooms are equally damaged.

175
00:07:51,408 --> 00:07:53,848
After accounting for coverage and data quality,

176
00:07:54,308 --> 00:07:56,488
the team found two opposing trends.

177
00:07:56,758 --> 00:07:59,028
Variants predicted to have the highest impact

178
00:07:59,028 --> 00:08:02,108
became less common, consistent with selection purging

179
00:08:02,108 --> 00:08:05,828
some of them. Meanwhile, moderately harmful variants

180
00:08:05,828 --> 00:08:09,898
accumulated, consistent with genetic drift outpacing selection.

181
00:08:10,368 --> 00:08:12,618
These are predictions about effects, not a

182
00:08:12,618 --> 00:08:14,958
direct medical examination of each mammoth.

183
00:08:15,358 --> 00:08:17,618
Across the sampled history, the team did

184
00:08:17,618 --> 00:08:20,728
not see a clear, accelerating genomic collapse.

185
00:08:21,108 --> 00:08:23,668
But their youngest genome predates the final

186
00:08:23,668 --> 00:08:27,508
generations. Demographic recovery and long-lasting genetic

187
00:08:27,508 --> 00:08:31,278
strain can coexist. Neither finding, on its

188
00:08:31,278 --> 00:08:33,468
own, names what killed the herd.

189
00:08:34,602 --> 00:08:36,032
Here is the missing witness.

190
00:08:36,542 --> 00:08:40,452
The 2024 genomic study had no samples from

191
00:08:40,452 --> 00:08:44,022
the population's final ten generations, roughly three

192
00:08:44,022 --> 00:08:47,242
centuries. Its youngest genome was dated to

193
00:08:47,242 --> 00:08:50,272
around forty three hundred calibrated years before

194
00:08:50,272 --> 00:08:53,222
present. That gap applies to the genome

195
00:08:53,222 --> 00:08:55,722
dataset; it does not mean there is

196
00:08:55,722 --> 00:08:57,432
no other evidence from the period.

197
00:08:57,932 --> 00:08:59,492
We can study the long setup.

198
00:08:59,922 --> 00:09:02,102
We cannot read a complete genetic record

199
00:09:02,102 --> 00:09:03,132
of the final chapter.

200
00:09:03,562 --> 00:09:05,322
There is also a newer kind of

201
00:09:05,322 --> 00:09:10,312
lead. In 2025, researchers studied ancient host-associated

202
00:09:10,312 --> 00:09:14,012
microbial DNA across hundreds of mammoth specimens,

203
00:09:14,432 --> 00:09:16,862
including material far older than the Wrangel

204
00:09:16,862 --> 00:09:20,192
population. Some bacteria were related to known

205
00:09:20,192 --> 00:09:23,652
pathogens. But related does not mean responsible.

206
00:09:24,062 --> 00:09:26,482
The study does not identify a disease

207
00:09:26,632 --> 00:09:29,182
that killed the Wrangel mammoths, and any

208
00:09:29,182 --> 00:09:30,952
health effects remain uncertain.

209
00:09:31,362 --> 00:09:33,032
The value of this research is the

210
00:09:33,032 --> 00:09:36,502
question it opens: what can carefully authenticated

211
00:09:36,502 --> 00:09:39,512
traces of microbes tell us alongside the

212
00:09:39,512 --> 00:09:43,102
animal's own DNA? For now, the diagnosis

213
00:09:43,102 --> 00:09:43,992
is still missing.

214
00:09:45,141 --> 00:09:46,691
So what can we actually say?

215
00:09:47,181 --> 00:09:51,241
Three columns.  Supported: the Wrangel population went

216
00:09:51,241 --> 00:09:52,671
through a severe bottleneck.

217
00:09:53,071 --> 00:09:56,331
The sampled genomes show reduced diversity and

218
00:09:56,331 --> 00:10:00,371
increased homozygosity, followed by a modelled demographic

219
00:10:00,371 --> 00:10:02,981
recovery. Genetic strain persisted.

220
00:10:03,451 --> 00:10:06,281
Isotope patterns were broadly stable in important

221
00:10:06,281 --> 00:10:09,751
dietary indicators, while other signals were interpreted

222
00:10:09,751 --> 00:10:12,031
as possible changes in water quality.

223
00:10:12,311 --> 00:10:16,091
Plausible, not proven: a severe icing event,

224
00:10:16,531 --> 00:10:20,901
disease, poorer water, human involvement, or some

225
00:10:20,901 --> 00:10:24,401
combination of pressures affecting a small isolated

226
00:10:24,401 --> 00:10:27,501
population. Each requires evidence we do not

227
00:10:27,501 --> 00:10:29,581
yet have.  Not established:

228
00:10:30,011 --> 00:10:32,711
a single named killer, a fatal winter

229
00:10:32,711 --> 00:10:35,271
directly recorded in the bones, or a

230
00:10:35,271 --> 00:10:37,131
genetic test proving the cause.

231
00:10:37,501 --> 00:10:40,111
The later genomic study challenges a simple

232
00:10:40,111 --> 00:10:43,631
account of inexorable collapse, but cannot tell

233
00:10:43,631 --> 00:10:45,541
us what happened in the missing final

234
00:10:45,541 --> 00:10:48,891
generations.  These categories are not rivals.

235
00:10:49,311 --> 00:10:51,991
A vulnerable population and a sudden event

236
00:10:52,341 --> 00:10:54,801
could work together. That is a hypothesis

237
00:10:54,801 --> 00:10:57,511
about compounding risks, not the answer to

238
00:10:57,511 --> 00:10:58,841
this particular case.

239
00:11:00,031 --> 00:11:02,101
Each witness tells us something different.

240
00:11:02,481 --> 00:11:05,811
The 2017 genome revealed potential genetic damage.

241
00:11:05,811 --> 00:11:08,741
The isotope record offered a picture of

242
00:11:08,741 --> 00:11:11,661
stable dietary signals, with a possible warning

243
00:11:11,661 --> 00:11:14,621
about water. Archaeology gives us the earliest

244
00:11:14,621 --> 00:11:17,071
known evidence of humans, not a complete

245
00:11:17,071 --> 00:11:21,751
attendance list. The 2024 genomes suggest that population

246
00:11:21,751 --> 00:11:24,541
recovery and genetic strain lasted together for

247
00:11:24,541 --> 00:11:27,051
thousands of years.  The mistake would be

248
00:11:27,051 --> 00:11:28,871
to pick the most dramatic clue and

249
00:11:28,871 --> 00:11:30,531
declare the mystery solved.

250
00:11:30,811 --> 00:11:33,451
A small gene pool, a changing environment,

251
00:11:33,891 --> 00:11:35,991
and an unlucky season might interact.

252
00:11:36,701 --> 00:11:38,621
But a plausible story is not the

253
00:11:38,621 --> 00:11:39,851
same thing as proof.

254
00:11:40,251 --> 00:11:43,101
The same distinction matters for living populations

255
00:11:43,101 --> 00:11:45,191
that are small and isolated today.

256
00:11:45,581 --> 00:11:48,451
A rebound in numbers can be encouraging

257
00:11:48,451 --> 00:11:50,601
without erasing every genetic risk.

258
00:11:50,831 --> 00:11:54,181
Protecting connections between populations may help give

259
00:11:54,181 --> 00:11:56,371
them more options when conditions change.

260
00:11:56,791 --> 00:11:59,221
For the last Wrangel mammoths, the ending

261
00:11:59,221 --> 00:12:00,851
remains a gap in the file.

262
00:12:01,341 --> 00:12:03,181
We know more about the long journey

263
00:12:03,501 --> 00:12:04,541
than the final moment.

264
00:12:04,851 --> 00:12:06,571
And the last question is not just

265
00:12:06,571 --> 00:12:08,891
what killed them. It is what evidence

266
00:12:08,891 --> 00:12:09,901
we are still missing.
